}$$ of connected, cubic graphs with\n\n$$ $\\mathop{\\mathrm{lim}}\\limits_{k\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\gamma ({G}_{k})}{| V({G}_{k})| }\\ge \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{69}.$$$\n\nSubsequently, Kelmans [10] constructed an infinite series of 2-connected, cubic graphs $ ${H}_{k}$$ with\n\n$$ $\\mathop{\\mathrm{lim}}\\limits_{k\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\gamma ({H}_{k})}{| V({H}_{k})| }\\ge \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{60}.$$$\n\nThus, there exist connected cubic graphs $ $G$$ of arbitrarily large order $ $n$$ satisfying $ $\\gamma (G)\\ge (\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{60})n$$. All known counterexamples to Reed's conjecture, including the above constructions of Kostochka and Stodolsky [9] and Kelmans [10], contain small cycles. Much discussion has centered on when Reed's conjecture becomes true with the additional condition that the girth, $ $g$$, of the graph is sufficiently large, where the girth is the length of a shortest cycle in $ $G$$. The first such result was presented by Kawarabayashi, Plummer, and Saito [11].\n\nTheorem 2. (([[11]]))If $ $G$$ is a connected cubic graph of order $ $n$$ and girth $ $g\\ge 3$$ has a 2-factor, then\n\n$$ $\\gamma (G)\\le \\left(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{9\\lfloor g/3\\rfloor +3}\\right)n.$$$\n\nRefining the ideas and techniques from Reed's seminal paper [5], and using intricate discharging arguments, Kostochka and Stodolsky [7] improved the upper bound in Theorem 2.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndependent Domination in Cubic Graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/283751864_Independent_Domination_in_Cubic_Graphs)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; the domination number of a subcubic graph. ... contradicting the fact that G is counterexample to Theorem 5. ... It is conjectured in [2] that if G /∈ {K 3,3, C 5✷K 2}is a connected cubic graph on n vertices, then ... result, we deduce the maint part of Reed’s [9] important result that if G is an n ve